---

<GuidePage title='Introduction - App Router Guide' root='..'>
  <div class='kicker'>Chapter 1</div>
  <h1>Introduction</h1>
  <p class='lede'>
    Don't fight the framework. This chapter is why that sentence is the whole guide in miniature:
    what's at stake when we do fight it, why server-first is the operating assumption rather than a
    preference, and where this document came from.
  </p>

  <h2 id='stakes'>The stakes</h2>
  <p>
    The architecture and approach you use <em>now</em> sets the stage for the rest of the product's life.
    That's not a platitude; it's the compounding math of a codebase. Every new page copies the patterns
    of the page before it. Every hook written around a client-side fetch becomes a dependency of the next
    feature. Conventions harden in months; unwinding them takes years.
  </p>
  <p>
    I say this as someone who has tried to refactor ojl-tracker out of exactly these problems,
    repeatedly. It is a never-ending battle, and the debt was cheapest on the day it was created. We
    do not want a re-Tracker: imagine how much more confusing the Slack channels, the product names,
    and the team names would become.
  </p>

  <Callout kind='alert' title='Server-first is not optional'>
    <p>
      Let's be unambiguous about this, because everything else in the guide depends on it. A
      server-first mentality is
      <strong
        >not a preference, not a style, and not one architecture among several we could reasonably
        choose</strong
      >. It is the operating assumption of React 19 and the App Router: the contract every primitive
      in this stack is built against. Every one of them assumes you are already thinking this way,
      so when you aren't, nothing throws an error; it just quietly works worse. The framework
      doesn't offer "SPA mode" as a supported alternative; it offers silence. Build against the
      grain and nothing stops you: the page renders, the demo works, the sprint closes. What you've
      actually done is taken out a loan no one issues a statement for.
    </p>
    <p>
      So the question is
      <strong
        >never whether building the old way will cause problems, only when they surface, and how
        much product is sitting on top of them by then</strong
      >. And when they surface, the menu is short and every item on it is expensive: hand-roll
      custom machinery that dampens the symptoms without ever eliminating them; accept the
      degradation as a permanent casualty and ship around it; retroactively refactor large portions
      of the UI, which is an order of magnitude harder than building it correctly was; or rewrite.
      There is no fifth option where it quietly works out. The mental-model shift this guide
      describes is the requirement; the only choice we get is whether we make it now, deliberately
      and cheaply, or later, under duress, at whatever the price has grown to.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <Callout kind='voice' title='The good news'>
    <p>
      You're smart, and you'll figure this out. None of this is beyond anyone on this team: it's a
      mental-model swap, not a skill gap. But it has to be deliberate: if we keep building into this
      thing without understanding how the technology wants to be used, we will end up in a world of
      pain that gets harder to escape every sprint. The whole point of this document is to make the
      deliberate path the easy one.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id='dont-fight'>Don't fight the framework</h2>
  <p>
    Next.js is not a rendering engine you bolt a React app onto. It is an opinionated architecture
    with a specific model of how pages should be composed, where data should be fetched, and where
    interactivity should live. When you work <em>with</em> that model, an enormous amount of complexity
    simply disappears: loading states, request timing, cache invalidation, bundle management, and layout
    stability are all problems the framework has first-class answers for. When you work <em
      >against</em
    > it, you don't just lose those answers; you actively re-create the problems they were designed to
    solve, and then you hand-roll worse solutions to them.
  </p>
  <p>
    The reason to work with the model is not deference; it is mechanics.
    <strong>React 19 and the App Router are deeply opinionated, and the App Router more so.</strong> Not
    opinionated for their own sake; opinionated because these are complex systems, and
    <strong
      >complex systems only deliver their guarantees when they can make assumptions about how you
      use them</strong
    >. The compiler can only optimize components that follow the rules of React. The router can only
    cache and stream what the server rendered. The boundary between server and client only pays off
    if it sits where the platform expects it. Every deviation from how the platform wants something
    done makes the machinery less effective, and past a point, <strong>actively destructive</strong
    >: the framework starts working against you, re-fetching what it would have cached, blocking
    what it would have streamed, shipping what it would have kept on the server.
    <strong>The opinions are the price of the guarantees.</strong>
  </p>
  <p>
    That is the trap this guide exists to keep us out of. The patterns we've all internalized from
    years of traditional React (fetch in an effect, hold it in state, show a spinner, lift state up,
    pass callbacks down) are not neutral habits that happen to be suboptimal here. In the App Router
    they are
    <strong>load-bearing mistakes</strong>: each one forecloses a framework capability, and the
    foreclosures compound.
  </p>

  <Callout kind='warn' title='What it looks like when it compounds'>
    <p>
      Pages that take visibly long to navigate to, in production, on real networks. Spinners nested
      inside spinners. Content that jumps as each request lands. And the insidious part: you
      progressively
      <em>lose the ability to adopt the tools designed to fix these problems</em>. Streaming can't
      help a page whose data loads after hydration. Caching can't help a request the server never
      sees. Partial prerendering can't help a tree that's client-rendered from the root. The further
      you build into the wrong model, the more expensive the road back; the refactoring becomes a
      never-ending battle.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id='what-this-is'>What this is, and why I wrote it</h2>
  <p>
    Start with what this document is made of. It is stitched together from the official Next.js and
    React documentation, reorganized and then elaborated: taking what the docs say and explaining
    what those things actually mean, in more detail, with concrete code and diagrams. The docs
    mention genuinely important design patterns briefly, sometimes in a single sentence near the end
    of a paragraph, without the why, the how, or an example. This guide slows down on exactly those
    and expands them into patterns you can actually build from.
  </p>
  <p>
    The rest of it is a second kind of pattern: the ones the docs never quite name. Work with the
    App Router the right way for long enough (server as the default, the client only where it's
    earned) and a set of design patterns starts surfacing on its own, through nothing but repetition
    and correction. Then comes the strange part: once they've surfaced, you start recognizing them
    everywhere: half-described in the docs, embedded in other codebases, implied by API shapes that
    suddenly make sense. Those patterns are written down here up front, because the surfacing takes
    months and the reading takes an evening.
  </p>
  <p>
    That second kind is the part I care about most. I started wrestling with the App Router back in
    2021, and it was a long, unglamorous slog of trial and error before I understood how it actually
    wants to be used. Most of the patterns in this guide are the residue of that process: the things
    I wish someone had handed me up front, instead of making me find them one painful refactor at a
    time. I can't give you the intuition (that part you still have to earn), but I can shorten the
    road, and that is the whole ambition here.
  </p>
  <Callout kind='voice' title='A useful way to hold it'>
    <p>
      Here is the mindset that makes all of this click, and it comes in two steps. The model is
      TypeScript: if you treat every type coercion as the worst thing in the world, an error to be
      hunted down and never tolerated, you will become very good at TypeScript.
    </p>
    <p>
      Apply that first to <code>useEffect</code>. Treat every effect as something that should make
      you wince, a cost to be justified rather than a tool to reach for, and you are forced to
      stretch the muscles that find another way: derive it during render, move it into the event
      handler, ask the server. That stretching is what makes you better at <em>React itself</em>;
      the library's own design patterns start surfacing on their own, and the docs' "you might not
      need an effect" guidance stops reading like advice and starts reading like the obvious move.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then apply the same treatment to <code>"use client"</code>. Treat every directive as a small
      defeat, avoid it like the plague, and the brain starts down the server-first paths instead:
      pushing the boundary lower, splitting along capability seams, asking why the server can't
      finish this. Stretch those muscles long enough and the design patterns of React 19 and the App
      Router emerge naturally, through nothing more than the trial and error of avoidance. They are
      the same patterns this guide hands you up front, to save you the months it takes to notice
      them.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p>
    One honest caveat before we go further. No single document, and no one-hour tech collab, can
    cover everything about server-first React, the App Router, and React 19; the surface is too
    large and it moves too fast. This guide is a map and a set of patterns, not a replacement for
    the docs or for your own experimentation. You will still have to put in the reps. The goal is
    only to make those reps land faster and sting less.
  </p>

  <h2 id='read-the-docs'>Read the docs</h2>
  <p>
    Seriously:
    <a href='https://nextjs.org/docs' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>read them</a>. Not as API
    reference when something breaks, but as a description of a model. The App Router docs are
    unusually good at explaining <em>why</em> each convention exists: why layouts don't re-render, why
    <code>loading.tsx</code> is a file and not a prop, why caching is opt-in, why the client boundary
    is a directive. Most of the patterns in this guide are not clever inventions; they are the documented,
    intended usage. The single biggest upgrade available to us as a team is to stop treating this stack
    like the React we already know and to step outside the traditional model we've all become accustomed
    to.
  </p>
  <p>
    This guide is the map, not the territory. Every chapter links to the primary sources, official
    Next.js and React documentation, collected in
    <a href='21-references.html'>§21 References</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
    And read both of them, not just Next.js. The React documentation (react.dev) is actually quite
    thorough, and a surprising amount of what feels like Next.js magic is really just React 19 doing
    its job; you will understand the framework far better once you understand the library underneath
    it. The division of labor between the two is worth internalizing: React explains the primitives
    in depth, and Next.js explains how they compose into an application, often tersely. When the two
    seem to disagree, they usually don't; you are just missing the React-level reason the
    Next.js-level rule exists.
  </p>
  <p>
    There is a specific reward for pushing through the terse parts. If you commit to server-first
    thinking and force your brain down those paths through trial and error until you start getting
    it right, the design patterns begin to emerge on their own. And then something useful happens:
    the next time you hit one of those single-sentence pronouncements in the Next.js docs, it
    finally lands, because you have already felt the problem it was quietly solving. Much of this
    guide is an attempt to front-load that click, to explain the why and the how behind the terse
    lines so you spend less time confused. It will not remove the process; nothing does. It just
    makes it less painful.
  </p>

  <h2 id='direction'>The direction of travel</h2>
  <p>
    "Don't fight the framework" carries more weight once you see that the framework is a moving
    object, and that it has been moving in one direction for over a decade. For roughly ten years
    (call it 2013 to 2020), "a React app" meant one thing: the server sends an empty HTML shell and
    a JavaScript bundle; the bundle boots; components mount; effects fire; data is fetched; the page
    assembles itself in the browser. Everything we internalized as "how React works"
    (fetch-in-effect, loading flags in state, lifting state up, client routers, spinners) is
    machinery for surviving inside that model.
  </p>
  <p>
    But that model was a stop on the road, not the destination, and the history makes the trajectory
    hard to argue with. Trace the actual events:
  </p>

