  <p>
    Read the second half of that timeline the way the React and Next.js teams read it:
    <em>every major primitive shipped since December 2020 assumes a server-first architecture.</em>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>React Server Components</strong> moved rendering (and with it, data access) to the server,
      making "component that runs in the browser" the explicitly-marked exception rather than the assumption.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Actions</strong> moved mutations to the server, making "POST to an API route, then juggle
      client state" unnecessary for the common case.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Suspense and streaming</strong> moved loading states out of components and into the tree
      structure, making "this component shows its own spinner" an anti-pattern rather than a convention.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Next.js 16's caching model</strong> (opt-in caching, partial prerendering by default when
      enabled) is built on the assumption that most of a page is server-rendered and cacheable, with dynamic
      regions marked by Suspense, an assumption an SPA-style app violates on every page.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The React Compiler</strong> removes the memoization busywork that made "just put everything
      in one big client component" feel pragmatic.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    None of them help (several literally cannot function) in a tree that is client-rendered from the
    root. The feature-level details live in
    <a href='20-new-primitives.html'>§20</a>; what matters here is the shape of the history. The
    client era's chronic problems (bundles that grow forever, request waterfalls, loading-state
    choreography, caches the server never sees) were not solved
    <em>inside</em> the client model. They were solved by leaving it. So writing SPA-style code in the
    App Router isn't "sticking with classic React." It is building on the legacy model of a platform that
    has already left, betting the product's future against the roadmap of every tool we build on.
  </p>
  <p>
    One entry on that timeline deserves a closer look, because it explains something people often
    read as coincidence: why React's evolution and Next.js's evolution move in lockstep. <strong
      >The React core team is no longer just Meta.</strong
    > In December 2021, Vercel hired Sebastian Markbåge (the architect of React Server Components and
    much of Suspense) explicitly to keep working on React from inside the company that builds Next.js
    (<a href='https://vercel.com/blog/supporting-the-future-of-react' target='_blank' rel='noopener'
      >"Supporting the Future of React"</a
    >), and more core members followed over the next two years: Andrew Clark, who led React 18's
    concurrent rendering, among them. Check
    <a href='https://react.dev/community/team' target='_blank' rel='noopener'
      >React's own team page</a
    >
    today: a substantial share of the core team draws a Vercel paycheck. The consequence is structural,
    not cosmetic. React 19 and the App Router weren't developed in parallel and reconciled later; they
    were <strong>co-developed</strong>, with Next.js serving as RSC's reference implementation. So
    the App Router isn't "Vercel's interpretation of where React is going." It is co-authored by the
    people who decide where React goes, which is exactly why betting against its model is betting
    against React itself.
  </p>
  <p>
    And none of this happened quietly. The people building the platform have been saying it out
    loud, on stage and in writing, for over a decade. If you want the shift from the primary
    sources, these are the ones worth your time:
  </p>
  <table class='tbl'>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th style='width: 30%'>When &amp; who</th>
        <th>What they said</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <strong>Nov 2014</strong>: Guillermo Rauch (two years later: creator of Next.js)
        </td>
        <td>
          <a
            href='https://rauchg.com/2014/7-principles-of-rich-web-applications'
            target='_blank'
            rel='noopener'>"7 Principles of Rich Web Applications"</a
          >: before Next.js existed, principle #1 was already
          <strong>"server rendered pages are not optional."</strong> The stack we're using is this essay,
          industrialized.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Mar 2018</strong>: Dan Abramov (React core), JSConf Iceland</td>
        <td>
          <a
            href='https://legacy.reactjs.org/blog/2018/03/01/sneak-peek-beyond-react-16.html'
            target='_blank'
            rel='noopener'>"Beyond React 16"</a
          >, the first public Suspense demo:
          <strong>async UI as the framework's job</strong>, not a loading flag in every component.
          The doctrine of <a href='11-suspense-streaming.html'>§11</a>, six years early.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <strong>Dec 2020</strong>: Dan Abramov &amp; Lauren Tan, with Sebastian Markbåge's RFC
          (React core)
        </td>
        <td>
          <a
            href='https://react.dev/blog/2020/12/21/data-fetching-with-react-server-components'
            target='_blank'
            rel='noopener'>"Data Fetching with React Server Components"</a
          >, the talk and RFC that <strong>reset the entire roadmap</strong>: rendering returns to
          the server, at zero bundle cost. Everything since builds on this hour.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Jan 2024</strong>: Dan Abramov, overreacted.io</td>
        <td>
