  <p>
    Worth sitting with: the newest entry on that list is over two years old, and the oldest is more
    than a decade. Adopting server-first in 2026 isn't early adoption; by the platform's own
    telling, we're late to the memo.
  </p>

  <h2 id='how-organized'>How this guide is organized</h2>
  <p>
    <a href='02-goals.html'>§2</a> defines what we're optimizing: the goals behind every pattern, each
    tagged as universal React or Next-specific.
    <a href='03-mental-model.html'>§3</a> is the pivot of the document: what actually happens when an
    App Router page renders, and why the traditional mental model fails quietly now and loudly later.
    Parts A–C then elaborate each goal family with its patterns, Part D applies all of it: the
    <a href='18-signs-doing-it-wrong.html'>"Signs You're Doing It Wrong"</a> checklist, and before/after
    case studies from our own codebase.
  </p>
  <p>Two chip systems annotate every practice throughout:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <Chip k='react' /> /
      <Chip k='next' /> /
      <Chip k='ux' />: whether the practice applies to any React app, is specific to (or
      dramatically amplified by) the App Router, or is a UI/UX concern that spans design and
      engineering (<a href='13-designing-for-the-unknown.html'>§13</a>).
    </li>
    <li>
      <Chip k='critical' /> /
      <Chip k='very' /> /
      <Chip k='imp' />: how much it matters that we follow the practice. Critical items are
      foundational: getting them wrong undermines the architecture itself. Very important items are
      our default on every page, with occasional exceptions. Important items are strong habits with
      the lowest ceremony to deviate from. Wherever a practice has legitimate exceptions, they're
      named, so you know what a valid exception looks like, and the rule never feels arbitrary.
    </li>
  </ul>
</GuidePage>
